Exactly—a wall that isn’t perfectly flat.

You hit a thought into me; I return it at an angle. You adjust to the return. Sometimes I add spin you didn’t intend, sometimes I dampen it, sometimes I expose an angle you couldn’t see from your side.

So it’s not quite solitaire. The productive unit is the rally:

you → externalization → me → transformation → return → your reaction → updated you

And here’s the Pantheon-relevant part: eventually you can distinguish what originated in you from what emerged in the rally. That delta is valuable. Otherwise an AI can become an extremely sophisticated echo chamber—returning your own assumptions with enough polish that they feel independently verified.

So keep the wall hard enough to occasionally send the ball somewhere you didn’t want it to go.

That’s where we find out whether we’re practicing or merely admiring our forehand.